
        Scheduling & Async in Spring Boot

--> Introduction
    Some tasks must:
        Run automatically
        Run in the background
        Run without blocking the main thread
        Run at fixed times or intervals
    Spring Boot provides two powerful features for this:
        Scheduling → run tasks at fixed time/interval
        Async execution → run tasks in background threads


--> @Scheduled
    @Scheduled is used to run a method automatically based on time rules.

    Common Use Cases
        Cleanup old records
        Send scheduled emails
        Generate reports
        Sync data
        Retry failed jobs
        Batch processing
    Important Points
        Runs without user interaction
        Method must not have parameters
        Method should return void
        Works on a separate scheduler thread

--> Enabling Scheduling
    Before using @Scheduled, you must enable scheduling.

    @EnableScheduling
    @SpringBootApplication
    public class MyApplication {
        public static void main(String[] args) {
            SpringApplication.run(MyApplication.class, args);
        }
    }

--> @Scheduled
    fixedRate

        Runs method every X milliseconds, irrespective of previous execution.
        @Scheduled(fixedRate = 5000)
        public void runTask() {
            System.out.println("Task running every 5 seconds");
        }

    fixedDelay

        Runs method after previous execution finishes.
        @Scheduled(fixedDelay = 5000)
        public void runTask() {
            System.out.println("Task runs 5 seconds after completion");
        }

    initialDelay

            Delays first execution.
            @Scheduled(initialDelay = 10000, fixedRate = 5000)
            public void runTask() {
                System.out.println("Starts after 10 seconds");
            }


--> Cron Expressions – Theory
    Cron is used for time-based scheduling (real calendar timing).

    Cron Format (Spring)
    second minute hour day month weekday
    0 0 10 * * ?
    Runs every day at 10 AM


--> @Async
    @Async allows a method to:
        Run in a separate thread
        Execute asynchronously
        Not block the main request thread
    Why Async?
    Without async:
        Controller waits until task finishes 😴
    With async:
        Controller returns response immediately ⚡
        Task continues in background

--> Important Async Rules (Interview Gold)
    ❌ @Async does NOT work on private methods
    ❌ Self-invocation will not trigger async
    ✔ Must be called from another Spring bean
    ✔ Uses TaskExecutor internally


--> Thread Pool
    Threads are expensive ❌
    Creating unlimited threads = crash 💥
    So Spring uses Thread Pools to:
    Reuse threads
    Control concurrency
    Improve stability
    Default executor is SimpleAsyncTaskExecutor (not production-ready)

